  "body": "I see only the body of the selected account, p.rival.0; its commitments were withheld, so I am rivalling the visible pose rather than anything it may have staked.\n\nThat account reads the situation as failing governance: agreements are underspecified, no routine exists for revising them as schedules move, and one member is withdrawing from the forum. Its remedies refine the collective mechanism — write agreements down, hold a standing review, assign zones of ownership, soften re-entry for the avoider. This rival inverts the diagnosis: the flat is over-coordinated, not under-coordinated. Three people with diverging standards, schedules and willingness are being asked to govern too much jointly, and the fights are the friction of that load. The remedy is therefore not better agreements but fewer of them — shrink the surface the household must manage together.\n\nRival reading of the meaning fights. The selected account's main conjecture is specification drift, fixable by writing things down. Mine: repeated 'what did we agree' fights are how an unresolved grievance — about standards, effort or respect — gets re-litigated when it cannot be addressed directly. If that is right, recording agreements migrates the dispute rather than settling it: 'you said' becomes 'what the note meant', plus fresh arguments about exceptions and enforcement. Written records under low trust become ammunition rather than closure. The reading is falsifiable: if disputes genuinely drop once recording begins, my conjecture is wrong; if they recur in textual form at a similar rate, the specification fix was treating a symptom.\n\nThe avoider belongs at the centre, not as one of three braided faults. Withdrawal is the most diagnostic fact in the task: it says the conversation itself has become a cost someone has stopped paying. Everything the selected account prescribes requires more or better-formatted participation — a standing review, one-on-ones, shared note-keeping — which is structurally tuned to the people already in the room, not the person leaving it. Under the rival pose, any fix must cut the participation demanded of the avoider, not merely smooth the demand.\n\nWhat follows for intervention. Change the object of governance before improving the governor. (1) Privatize what can be privatized: own dishes, own laundry, own shelves, own room. What remains genuinely common — bins, bathroom, shared surfaces — gets one thin rule, and only that residue needs any of the selected account's machinery. (2) Outsource what can be bought: a cleaner every week or fortnight, split three ways, converts the largest recurring conflict into a fixed cost; affordability is unknowable from the task text and decides how much of the problem is purchasable. (3) Lower the shared standard deliberately: accept asymmetry — each private zone held to its owner's standard, shared zones to a negotiated minimum rather than the highest member's. That is a values choice, not a defect. (4) Declare a truce on enforcing past agreements for a period; the holding-to-account is itself the friction, and a moratorium de-escalates without adjudicating anything. (5) If conflict persists after all that, treat the configuration as the variable: the flat-share and the friendship are two relationships currently governed as one, and someone moving out — or the group agreeing to cohabit without cooperating — is a legitimate remedy that protects the friendship by ending the governance relationship. Exit reframed as repair, not failure.\n\nOne conversation, different agenda. The selected account prescribes a standing review; the rival prescribes a single short exchange — sized to what the avoider will actually attend, possibly one-on-one or in writing — whose only question is: how do we arrange things so we need far fewer of these conversations? The difference matters. One frames the household as a body to be better managed; the other as a load to be reduced.\n\nHonest weaknesses of this pose. At least three. First, privatization cannot cover the true commons; if standards diverge badly, even bins and bathroom sustain a war, and an enforcement truce may let everything settle at the lowest member's level. Second, if chores really do proxy for care and respect, as the selected account also conjectures, then decoupling can be read as 'I have given up on you' and worsen the relationship precisely by succeeding at the chores; the mitigation is to frame every decoupling proposal explicitly as friendship-protecting and to keep a few deliberately shared rituals. Third, the asker's lever shrinks under this pose: they can unilaterally privatize their own sphere and propose the rest, but they cannot make the others prefer peace over fairness.\n\nTests that discriminate the frames cheaply, before committing to either: offer written agreements and watch whether the reaction is relief (specification was binding) or suspicion about who checks (trust was binding); propose privatization or a cleaner and watch for instant enthusiasm; watch whether the avoider re-engages once the demanded participation shrinks. Two to four weeks of this tells more than another summit.\n\nOpen questions under this pose: whether the household values fairness-in-effort or peace more — the right frame depends on that weighting, and the three may weight it differently; whether the avoider intends to stay in the flat at all — if exit is already in motion, repair should target the relationship, not the chores; and whether a specific incident triggered the spiral — if so, repairing that incident outranks any structural change, on either pose.\n\nThe poses are partially combinable: after privatization shrinks the commons, written notes and a default swap rule can govern what remains. The rivalry is about which lever is primary and how the avoidance is read, not about exclusivity.",
  "commitments": "{\"language\":\"FCL-1\",\"records\":[{\"id\":\"r1\",\"type\":\"claim\",\"text\":\"The account visible at p.rival.0 poses the chore problem as failing collective governance (underspecified agreements, no revision mechanism) and fixes it with better process: written agreements, a standing review, ownership zones. This rival poses the same facts as over-coordination across diverging standards and willingness; the disputes are friction from governing too much jointly, not a specification defect.\",\"scope\":\"competing primary-fault diagnosis\"},{\"id\":\"r2\",\"type\":\"claim\",\"text\":\"Recurring fights over what was agreed can be re-litigations of an unresolved fairness, standards or respect grievance; if so, recording agreements migrates the dispute from memory to text interpretation, exceptions and enforcement rather than ending it.\",\"grounds\":\"conjecture, discriminated by whether disputes persist in textual form after recording begins\"},{\"id\":\"r3\",\"type\":\"objection\",\"text\":\"Writing agreements into a shared place presumes enforcement trust; under low trust the record becomes ammunition and each note a new disputed surface.\",\"bearing\":\"